code_guidance:
Read GPIOA+GPIOB in one transaction with the built-in internal-address form, which emits exactly S/addrW/0x12/Sr/addrR/data/data/P:
  uint8_t n = Wire.requestFrom((uint8_t)addr, (uint8_t)2, (uint32_t)0x12, (uint8_t)1, (uint8_t)1);
  if (n == 2) { a = Wire.read(); b = Wire.read(); }
The equivalent long form is: Wire.beginTransmission(addr); Wire.write(0x12); if (Wire.endTransmission(false) != 0) -> error; Wire.requestFrom(addr, 2, true);

Add an application-level reentrancy guard around every Wire access, because Wire is not reentrant and endTransmission()/requestFrom() call yield() on every poll iteration:
  static volatile bool i2c_busy = false;
  // enter: if (i2c_busy) return I2C_BUSY; i2c_busy = true;  ... exit: i2c_busy = false;
Enter/exit with interrupts briefly masked (__disable_irq()/__enable_irq()) around the test-and-set.

Alternative, stronger fix if you do not need serialEvent dispatch during I2C: define your own yield in the sketch. It is declared weak, so this compiles and fully overrides the core version:
  extern "C" void yield(void) { }
The cost is that serialEvent()/serialEventN() are then never dispatched anywhere, so you must poll Serial1.available() in loop() yourself. At 921600 baud (~11 us/byte) a 132 us I2C transaction accumulates ~12 bytes, well inside the default HardwareSerial RX buffer.

Write your own public bus-recovery routine — the library's force_clock() is private, never emits a STOP, and only runs after a 16 ms stall. For Wire on pins 18/19:

bool i2cRecover(uint8_t sdaPin, uint8_t sclPin) {
  pinMode(sclPin, OUTPUT_OPENDRAIN); digitalWrite(sclPin, HIGH);
  pinMode(sdaPin, OUTPUT_OPENDRAIN); digitalWrite(sdaPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
    if (digitalRead(sdaPin) && digitalRead(sclPin)) break;   // released early
    digitalWrite(sclPin, LOW);  delayMicroseconds(5);
    digitalWrite(sclPin, HIGH); delayMicroseconds(5);
  }
  // manual STOP: SDA low while SCL high, then SDA high
  digitalWrite(sdaPin, LOW);  delayMicroseconds(5);
  digitalWrite(sclPin, HIGH); delayMicroseconds(5);
  digitalWrite(sdaPin, HIGH); delayMicroseconds(5);
  bool ok = digitalRead(sdaPin) && digitalRead(sclPin);
  Wire.begin(); Wire.setClock(400000);   // re-mux pins and restore speed
  return ok;
}

Note pinMode(pin, OUTPUT_OPENDRAIN) writes pad = DSE(7)|ODE (drive ~21 Ohm, NO internal pull-up, no hysteresis) and sets mux = ALT5|SION, so recovery relies entirely on your external pull-ups. Then Wire.begin() restores the LPI2C mux via configSDApin()/configSCLpin() — and the setClock() after it is mandatory.
